# ============================================================================
# WHAT NEVER GOES UP TO VERCEL
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE ITS ABSENCE HUNG A DEPLOY FOR 23 MINUTES.
# `vercel deploy` from this directory sat at status UNKNOWN with a 0ms build and
# produced no output at all. It was not building — it was still uploading:
#
#     .next          1.5 GB      (a local production build)
#     node_modules   503 MB
#     .git            27 MB
#
# Two gigabytes, over a domestic connection, for a source tree that is about
# 25 MB. Vercel installs dependencies and runs the build on its own machines, so
# every byte of the first two is not merely wasted, it is actively harmful: the
# uploaded `.next` can shadow the one the build produces.
#
# The failure mode is the nasty part — no error, no progress, just a deployment
# that never becomes Ready while the subdomain keeps serving the previous build.
# Which reads exactly like "the deploy worked and the fix did not".
#
# ⚠️ .gitignore ALREADY LISTED .next AND node_modules and it made no difference.
# Do not delete this file on the assumption the two are equivalent.
# ============================================================================

# Build output — Vercel produces its own.
.next/
out/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Installed on the build machine from the lockfile.
node_modules/

# History is not needed to build a deployment.
.git/

# ---- Local-only material ----------------------------------------------------
# Nothing secret should be here anyway (.gitignore covers these too), but a
# deploy upload is one more path out of this directory, so it is named again.
.env*
env*
.vercel/

# ---- Weight with no runtime purpose ----------------------------------------
# The 5 MB source artwork. The served copy lives in public/brand/ and the email
# mark is generated into lib/email/mark.ts — neither reads this folder.
logo/

# Generated previews, never imported.
.report-preview/
email-preview.html

# ⚠️ TESTS ARE DELIBERATELY *NOT* EXCLUDED HERE. Skipping `test/` and
# `**/__tests__/` would save about 2 MB out of 2 GB — nothing — while giving the
# build machine a source tree that differs from the one `tsc` runs against
# locally. A deploy that fails for a reason no local check reproduces costs far
# more than the bytes. The four entries above are 99.9% of the saving.
